Rob Ellwood wrote:
> IIRC, the code has partially implemented what I'll call
> "taint resistance".  This lets you eat tainted corpses safely.  If a
> healer without taint resistance tries to eat a corpse:
The code has "sickness resistance", that protects from Ill and FoodPois
statuses. As far as I know, the only way to obtain it in vanilla is to
polyself into a ghoul.

> Chance of	
> being tainted:	Message:
> 0%			"The [x] corpse is fresh.  Eat it?"
> <10%  		"The [x] corpse is almost fresh.  Eat it?"
> <50%			"The [x] corpse is going bad.  Eat it?"
> <99%			"The [x] corpse has probably gone bad.  Eat it?"
> 100%			"The [x] corpse is tainted. You don't eat it."
I don't see a reason why there should be a prompt at all at 0%; just eat
the corpse. (Unless this is meant to replace the "there is a corpse
here, eat it?" message, which doesn't appear in NH4 unless there are
multiple corpses on the square.)

NetHack 4 already has a warning for eating corpses that are dangerously
old (it kicks in at 30 turns, which is the minimum for a cursed corpse
to be tainted). Working out the exact probability, taking into account
the BCU of the corpse, would be an interesting buff for food appraisal,
and giving food appraisal to a role would be a potentially interesting
buff for that role.

I'm not sure this fits on Healer, though. There's no real flavour that
they should be better at eating raw meat; and they're already
differentiated to some extent via their guaranteed stone to flesh spell,
that gives unlimited food once it can be cast reliably (rocks aren't
exactly in short supply, and the resulting meatballs are useful for
training pets as well as for eating).

I'd actually argue that Healer is one of the better-differentiated
classes; it doesn't really play similarly to anything but Tourist early
on, and it doesn't really play like Tourist in the midgame. (Of course,
there are still only three or four ways of playing the endgame and all
but two suck, so role differentiation should probably focus on that
instead; and the change you mention here really doesn't do anything to
change the endgame, both because corpses are mostly irrelevant by then,
and because you have a unihorn anyway so there's no reason not to risk
eating a corpse.)

